# K3entropy

Companion code for the paper
"A Real K3 Automorphism with Most of Its Entropy in the Real Part"

This repository contains the code accompanying version 2 of the arXiv preprint
(arXiv:2506.03479v2). The code snapshot for that submission is the
[arxiv-v2 tag](https://github.com/ethanhcoo/K3entropy/tree/arxiv-v2). The
`main` branch may continue to change.

The code accompanying arXiv v1 is archived in the
[arxiv-v1 release](https://github.com/ethanhcoo/K3entropy/releases/tag/arxiv-v1).

## Rigorous C certification

Requirements:

- a C11 compiler (`cc` by default)
- GNU Make
- GMP
- GNU MPFR

The makefile honors the standard `CC`, `CPPFLAGS`, `LDFLAGS`, and `LDLIBS`
variables. For example, a Homebrew installation can be supplied without
editing the makefile:

```sh
make -C src \
  CPPFLAGS="-I$(brew --prefix)/include" \
  LDFLAGS="-L$(brew --prefix)/lib" \
  checkAllCertifications
```

Select a particular compiler in the same way, for example with `CC=clang`.

From the repository root, run:

```sh
make -C src checkAllCertifications
```

This compiles and runs the three rigorous C certification programs together
with their supporting unit tests. A successful run exits with status zero. If
any required strict inequality, interval-domain condition, or exact geometric
check cannot be certified, the relevant program exits with a nonzero status.

This command runs the C certificates only. The separate SageMath/Flipper
computation is described below. The path certification is the longest part
and may take several minutes.

## SageMath/Flipper certificate

The SageMath/Flipper computation is a separate exact certificate and is not
run by `make -C src checkAllCertifications`. The saved notebook output was
produced and checked with:

- SageMath 10.5
- Flipper 0.15.6

After installing SageMath 10.5, install Flipper into Sage's environment:

```sh
sage -pip install flipper==0.15.6
```

From the repository root, open the notebook and run all cells in order:

```sh
sage -n jupyter FlipperDilatation.ipynb
```

The notebook reads the mapping-class word from `mapping_class_word.txt` and
checks the SageMath and Flipper versions before running the computation.

The canonical word can also be checked directly against the word recomputed
from the certified arc-data by `mclass`:

```sh
make -C src checkMappingClassWord
```

This downstream consistency check is intentionally separate from the rigorous
C-certificate aggregate. If the manuscript source is available, its labeled
mapping-class equation can be checked against the same canonical word as well:

```sh
make -C src checkManuscriptWord MANUSCRIPT=/path/to/main_updated.tex
```

## Source layout

The source files are divided by purpose:

	src/certification/
		The three programs that certify the computer-assisted claims in the
		manuscript, together with the common code, data, and tests they use.

	src/illustration_and_exploration/
		Programs for producing figures, exploring numerical data, and computing
		the mapping-class word, together with their supporting code. These
		programs are not used for rigorous certification.

The makefile remains in `src/`. Generated executables are written to `bin/`,
which is created automatically and ignored by Git.

For an accessible visualization of the arc computation, begin with
`src/illustration_and_exploration/arcs.c`. It produces the plots and
exploratory arc-data that motivated Figure 4. The rigorous program
`src/certification/verifyArcs.c` independently constructs interval and
exact-rational certificates for the corresponding relative homotopy classes.

The plain `make all` target builds the programs but does not run the
certificates.

## Quick start for the illustration and exploration programs

From the repository root, build the programs in `src` and then run the desired
`.run` file from `example`. For example:

```sh
cd src
make
cd ../example
./Arcs.run
```

## Background and conventions

This repository adds functionality to Curt McMullen's *Orbits of
Automorphisms of K3 Surfaces*, Version 3.2 (3/2006). Much of the older
documentation below is adapted from his README.

THEORY.  A K3 surface S in R^3 is specified by the equation

(1+x^2)(1+y^2)(1+z^2) + Axyz = B

In many files, we set $A = 10$, $B = 2$. 
	warning: periodicExact, verifyPeriodicOrbit, derivativesExact, derivativesInterval, derivatives, and verifyArcs have $A = 10$, $B = 2$ hard-coded into some functions

A generic line parallel to the x, y or z axis meets S in 2 points, so we obtain 3 involutions by "sheet interchange".
These maps preserves a natural volume element on S.

Set f = Iz Iy Ix;

PROGRAMS.  The source files are described below.

RIGOROUS CERTIFICATION PROGRAMS (`certification/`).

`verifyPeriodicOrbit.c` — Appendix C.3
    Purpose:
        - Certifies the pseudo-orbit estimates used to apply the shadowing
          lemma.
        - Every finite decimal in the pseudo-orbit data is interpreted as
          the corresponding exact rational number.

    Certified conclusions:
        - For each i, the input and output discriminants are strictly
          positive.
        - The image of the ith pseudo-orbit point lies on the prescribed
          branch of the next coordinate chart; in particular, 0 lies in U_i.
        - The two coordinates of
              r_i = f_i^c(0)
          lie in rigorously computed intervals.
        - The directed bounds displayed in Table 2 are valid, and
              max_i ||r_i||_2 < 10^(-29).

    Inputs:
        - The exact pseudo-orbit coordinates and chart choices stored in
          `k3_orbit_data.c`.

    Output and failure behavior:
        - Prints the certified Table 2 bounds and the underlying
          full-precision interval enclosures.
        - Exits with nonzero status if any domain condition, branch
          condition, table bound, or strict inequality cannot be certified.


`derivativesInterval.c` — Appendix C.2
    Purpose:
        - Certifies the derivative estimates used in Sections 3.5.1
          and 3.5.2 by outward-rounded MPFR interval arithmetic.
        - The decimal entries of Tables 3 and 4 are interpreted as exact
          rational numbers.

    Certified conclusions:
        - The values in Table 3 are the correct roundings to two decimal
          places.
        - The constants defined in Section 3.5.1 satisfy
              K < 115,  R < 163,  M < 442.
        - The discriminant D is strictly positive on every axis-aligned
          square of radius 10^(-3) about a pseudo-orbit coordinate, and
          hence on the corresponding Euclidean ball.
        - If L_i = (Df_i^c)_0 and \widetilde L_i is the rational matrix
          displayed in Table 4, then every entry of
              L_i - \widetilde L_i
          has absolute value less than 10^(-2).

    Inputs:
        - The exact pseudo-orbit coordinates and chart choices stored in
          `k3_orbit_data.c`.
        - The exact decimal reference values for Tables 3 and 4 stored in
          `derivativesInterval.c`.

    Output and failure behavior:
        - Prints the full interval enclosures for the discriminant
          derivatives and the matrices L_i, together with the certified
          error margins.
        - Exits with nonzero status if any required strict inequality
          cannot be certified.


`verifyArcs.c` — Appendix C.4
    Purpose:
        - Independently certifies the relative homotopy classes represented
          in the top panel of Figure 4.
        - This is the rigorous counterpart of the exploratory plotting
          program `arcs.c`; it does not use floating-point output from
          `arcs.c` as proof input.

    Step 1:
        - Constructs pairwise disjoint rational boxes B_0,...,B_9 containing
          the ten true orbit points in stereographic coordinates.
        - Certifies their left-to-right order, the induced permutation of
          the orbit, avoidance of the stereographic projection point, and
          that the shadowing orbit has least period ten.

    Step 2:
        - For each straight segment joining consecutive puncture boxes,
          constructs an adaptive rational partition of [0,1] and a chain
          of overlapping rational rectangles enclosing its image under the
          explicit map Pi.
        - Certifies that the image is defined throughout, avoids all
          inappropriate puncture boxes, and begins and ends in the required
          endpoint boxes.
        - Constructs a rational polygonal representative through the
          rectangle chain.
        - Checks containment, embeddedness, and the transversality
          conditions of Definition 3.11 using exact rational arithmetic.
        - Computes the literal arc-data exactly and applies the
          homotopy-preserving relative reductions implemented in
          `arc_word.c`.

    Reference data:
        - `arc_certificate_data.c` stores the expected literal and reduced
          rows. These rows are not used to prove the interval enclosures or
          the rectangle-chain conditions. After a rational polygonal
          candidate has passed the analytic and exact-geometric checks, its
          independently computed literal and reduced words are compared
          with the stored rows.

    Inputs:
        - The exact pseudo-orbit coordinates and chart choices stored in
          `k3_orbit_data.c`.
        - The stored literal and reduced comparison rows in
          `arc_certificate_data.c`.

    Output and failure behavior:
        - Prints the certified puncture order, the exact polygonal arc-data,
          the reduced words used by `mclass.c`, and statistics for the
          interval subdivision.
        - Exits with nonzero status if any interval, domain, containment,
          embeddedness, transversality, or exact word comparison is
          inconclusive.

CERTIFICATION SUPPORT MODULES AND TESTS (certification/).

certificate_interval.c and certificate_interval.h
		- Shared outward-rounded MPFR interval arithmetic for the rigorous certificate programs
		- Each certification program explicitly selects its own precision
		- Operations return status values so the calling certification program can provide certificate-specific failure messages

k3_orbit_data.c and k3_orbit_data.h
		- Store the ten exact pseudo-orbit coordinate strings and chart choices in one canonical table
		- Used by verifyPeriodicOrbit.c, derivativesInterval.c, and verifyArcs.c

arc_certificate_data.c, arc_certificate_data.h,
	arc_word.c, and arc_word.h
		- arc_certificate_data.c stores both the literal unreduced position/height rows checked by verifyArcs.c and the reduced rows used by mclass.c
		- arc_word.c performs the relative word reduction and compares computed words with the stored position/height rows

testCertificateInterval.c, testK3OrbitData.c, and testArcWord.c
		- Test the shared interval-arithmetic functions, the stored orbit and chart data, and the arc-word normalization.

ILLUSTRATION AND EXPLORATION PROGRAMS (illustration_and_exploration/).

periodic.c
		- Experimentation only (No MPFR)
		- Useful for finding recurrent orbits

derivatives.c
		- Experimentation only (No MPFR)
		- Outputs derivative information along an orbit without using GNU MPFR library

arcs.c
		- This is the main program to read and run if you want to understand the arc computation and reproduce its plots and arc-data
		- Its numerical output provides the readable starting point for Figure 4; verifyArcs.c is the longer formal certification that the resulting arc-data are correct
	Inputs in arcs.c:
		- the x,y,z coordinates of a pseudo-periodic point and its period 
	Inputs in command line (edit in arcs.run):
		-b	Suppress plotted arc lines.
		-c [x y] -r [r]
			Set window for image by giving center and "radius".
			Default is center (0,0), radius 1.2.
		-e [eps]
			Set the accuracy of the arcs plot.
			The arc is drawn as a series of line segments each of length about eps.
		-p [A B]
			Change the parameters for the surface S.
		-q	Currently has no effect; diagnostic output remains enabled.
		-t	Currently has no effect on arc plotting.
		-s 	Rescales to unit sphere
		-u 	Rescales, stereographically projects to the plane, and maps coordinatewise to a stretched rectangle
		-w [i]
			Takes ith iteration under f of each arc p_k, i.e. f^i(p_k)
		-v [m] 
			Plots only f^i(p_m), the image of the mth arc. If this is missing, all arcs are plotted.
	Outputs
		- arc data for $f^i(p_k)$, where $i$ is fixed and $k$ varies over $0\leq k < orbit_length -1
		- PostScript is printed to stdout; arc-data and diagnostics are printed to stderr
	To generate the top panel of 'figure4':
		(i) Make sure that:
				marked_point.x = -1.726895448754858426328854724474;  
				marked_point.y = 1.041643093944314148360673792017;
				marked_point.z = 1.726895448754858426328854724474;	
		(ii) In Arcs.run: 
				../bin/arcs -r 3 -e .002 -p 10 2 -u -w 1| ps2pdf - > figure4.pdf 
				open figure4.pdf &
		(iii) To see each image arc individually, in Arcs.run:
				../bin/arcs -r 3 -e .0002 -p 10 2 -u -v 0  -w 1 | ps2pdf - > arc0.pdf 
				open arc0.pdf &
			and change arcs by changing the integer following '-v' to anything 0-8

mclass.c
		- Exact combinatorial computation from the certified reduced arc-data.
		  It is not one of the interval-certification programs.
		- Implements the algorithm of Section 3.6.1 and generates the words
		  g_i displayed in Figure 4.
		- From the resulting word g, it also produces the machine-readable word
		  for [f^2] = [\widehat g^{-1} \circ \overline g].
		- The proof does not require correctness of the implementation itself:
		  the individual words g_i can be checked directly from Figure 4 by
		  tracing the indicated half-twists.

		Input:
			- The certified reduced arc-data stored in
			  `arc_certificate_data.c`.

		Outputs:
			- The words g_i and g in Dehn half-twist generators.
			- The words for \widehat g^{-1}, \overline g, and f^2.
			- With `--flipper-word`, only the canonical machine-readable
			  Flipper word.

`mapping_class_word.txt`
- The canonical 250-generator Flipper word representing [f^2].
- The symbols `s_i` and `S_i` represent s_i and s_i^{-1},
  respectively.
- `make -C src checkMappingClassWord` compares this file with the word
  recomputed by `mclass`.
- `make -C src checkManuscriptWord MANUSCRIPT=...` compares the same
  canonical word with the labeled equation in the LaTeX source.

`FlipperDilatation.ipynb`
- Reads `mapping_class_word.txt` and constructs the corresponding
  Flipper mapping class.
- Computes its Nielsen--Thurston type and dilatation.
- Prints the exact minimal polynomial and a certified isolating
  interval.
- Constructs the 42 x 42 integral hitting matrix and checks the stated
  characteristic-polynomial factorization.

k3.c and k3.h
		- k3.c contains some functions used in other files.
	        - k3.h declares these functions and sets error thresholds.

ps.c
		- Contains PostScript plotting and coordinate-transformation helpers used by the illustration programs.

LEGACY AND REFERENCE PROGRAMS.

illustration_and_exploration/periodicExact.c
		- Legacy high-precision reference, provided certification in earlier version of the paper, superseded for certification by verifyPeriodicOrbit.c
		- Checks whether a particular orbit is a delta-pseudo-orbit for fixed delta, using the GNU MPFR library
	Inputs: All inputs to periodicExact happen within periodicExact.c
		- Specify a_i, b_i (see Arxiv preprint for notation) where 0\leq i < porbit_length
		- Specify delta
	Outputs:
		- Prints 'successful delta pseudo-orbit' to stderr if for all i, d(\phi_i^c(a_i, b_i), (a_{i+1 (mod porbit_length)}, b_{i+1 (mod porbit_length)})) <= delta
		- Prints 'failure' to stderr otherwise; currently exits with status 0 in either case
		Note: Order of operations in function definitions for [alpha], [beta], [p_{\pm}], [f], [f_i^c] agree with order used in the Appendix of Arxiv preprint to produce error estimates.

illustration_and_exploration/derivativesExact.c
		- Legacy high-precision point evaluation, superseded for certification by derivativesInterval.c
		- Gives derivative information for the specific pseudo-orbit above using the GNU MPFR library (see Tables 2 and 3 of Arxiv preprint)
	Inputs: All inputs to derivativesExact happen within derivativesExact.c
		- Specify a_i,b_i
	Outputs:
		- Prints derivatives to command line (stderr)

See the individual .c files for comments describing specific functions

EXAMPLES.

The .run files in example/ can be run using the procedure from QUICKSTART.

An example of a arcs.run is:
	../bin/arcs -r 3 -e .0002 -p 10 2 -u -v 0  -w 1 | ps2pdf - > arc0.pdf 
	open arc0.pdf &


TROUBLESHOOTING

ARCS
	IF: 'path length exceeded N'
	THEN: increase eps, increase MaxPathlength

	IF: 'trouble assigning start/end point'
	THEN: input point is not sufficiently pseudo-periodic
